    <md:title>9.9.1: Finding the Vertex in Vertex Form</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ecbad763-a271-4559-9d72-a403d64ae6f3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <!--Q#1-->
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para> Write the quadratic function \(f(x)=x^2+6x+13\) in vertex form.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\(f(x)=(x+3)^2+4\)</para>
            <para>\((\frac{b}{2})^2=(\frac{6}{2})^2=\frac{36}{4}=9\)
    \(f(x)=x^2+6x+9+13-9\)
    \(f(x)=x^2+6x+9+4\)
    \(f(x)=(x+3)^2+4\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Using the vertex form of the quadratic found in question 1, what is the vertex?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para/>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((-3, 4)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#-->
